WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.326
A class becomes especially useful when we

00:00:02.353 --> 00:00:04.932
create multiple objects from the same blueprint

00:00:05.855 --> 00:00:09.408
Imagine a Product class The Product class

00:00:09.421 --> 00:00:12.066
might define two attributes name and price

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.763
It might also define a method that

00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:17.908
calculates a sale price From that one

00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:21.434
class we can create many objects One

00:00:21.461 --> 00:00:23.553
object could represent a laptop with a

00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:26.987
price of nine hundred dollars Another object

00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:28.974
could represent a monitor with a price

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:32.066
of three hundred dollars A third object

00:00:32.092 --> 00:00:34.289
could represent a keyboard and so on

00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:36.934
All of these objects follow the same

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:38.842
structure because they come from the same

00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:42.605
Product class However each object stores its

00:00:42.618 --> 00:00:45.618
own values This is similar to using

00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:47.789
the same blank form for many records

00:00:48.711 --> 00:00:50.658
The form has the same fields but

00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:54.842
each completed form contains different information
Methods

00:00:54.855 --> 00:00:56.592
also work with the values stored in

00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:59.592
each object If the Product class has

00:00:59.618 --> 00:01:01.934
a sale price method the laptop object

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:04.382
uses the laptop's price while the monitor

00:01:04.395 --> 00:01:08.289
object uses the monitor's price This separation

00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:11.645
is important The class provides the common

00:01:11.658 --> 00:01:17.263
blueprint The objects represent individual instances
In

00:01:17.289 --> 00:01:19.474
analytics this pattern can be useful when

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.382
we want to model repeated entities such

00:01:21.395 --> 00:01:25.947
as customers products patients locations
transactions experiments

00:01:26.132 --> 00:01:27.605
or model configurations
